🚀 Tips and Tricks for Binary Watch
Bit Counting: In Java, Integer.bitCount(int i) is a highly optimized intrinsic method that returns the number of one-bits. Knowing this standard library method saves you from writing a helper function to count bits.
String Formatting: While String.format("%d:%02d", h, m) is readable, it is relatively slow in Java. Using StringBuilder (as shown in the solution above) is faster and often preferred in competitive programming contexts.
Bounds Check:
Max Hour: 11 (1011 binary) 3 bits on.
Max Minute: 59 (111011 binary) 5 bits on.
Therefore, the maximum possible turnedOn value that returns a valid time is . If the input is 9 or 10, you can immediately return an empty list.

Code:-
class Solution {
    public List<String> readBinaryWatch(int turnedOn) {
        List<String> times = new ArrayList<>();


        for(int h = 0;h < 12;h++){
            
            for(int m = 0; m < 60;m++){
                if(Integer.bitCount(h) + Integer.bitCount(m) == turnedOn)
                    // times.add(String.format("%d:%02d", h, m));
                    times.add(h + ":" + (m < 10 ? "0" + m : m));
            }
        }
        return times;
    }
}
